{
  "id": "d65025cf-8bf1-454f-9507-55890ab9f88c",
  "kind": "Theorem",
  "definition_keys": [],
  "heading": null,
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{Direct Product of Groups is a Group}",
  "body_text": null,
  "proof_text": "Let $G_1 = (X_1, *_1, e_1)$ and $G_2 = (X_2, *_2, e_2)$ be groups.\nWe verify the group axioms for the direct product $G_1 \\times G_2$:\n1. **Associativity**: Let $(a_1, a_2), (b_1, b_2), (c_1, c_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2$.\n   By definition of the coordinatewise operation $*_3$:\n   $$ ((a_1, a_2) *_3 (b_1, b_2)) *_3 (c_1, c_2) = (a_1 *_1 b_1, a_2 *_2 b_2) *_3 (c_1, c_2) = ((a_1 *_1 b_1) *_1 c_1, (a_2 *_2 b_2) *_2 c_2). $$\n   By the associativity of $*_1$ in $G_1$ and $*_2$ in $G_2$, this equals:\n   $$ (a_1 *_1 (b_1 *_1 c_1), a_2 *_2 (b_2 *_2 c_2)) = (a_1, a_2) *_3 (b_1 *_1 c_1, b_2 *_2 c_2) = (a_1, a_2) *_3 ((b_1, b_2) *_3 (c_1, c_2)). $$\n2. **Identity**: The element $e_3 = (e_1, e_2)$ satisfies:\n   $$ (a_1, a_2) *_3 (e_1, e_2) = (a_1 *_1 e_1, a_2 *_2 e_2) = (a_1, a_2), $$\n   and symmetrically $(e_1, e_2) *_3 (a_1, a_2) = (a_1, a_2)$.\n3. **Inverses**: For any $(a_1, a_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2$, the element $(a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1})$ satisfies:\n   $$ (a_1, a_2) *_3 (a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1}) = (a_1 *_1 a_1^{-1}, a_2 *_2 a_2^{-1}) = (e_1, e_2) = e_3, $$\n   and $(a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1}) *_3 (a_1, a_2) = (e_1, e_2) = e_3$.\nThus $(G_1 \\times G_2, *_3, e_3)$ is a group.",
  "proof_source": "Proof: \"\"\"\nLet $G_1 = (X_1, *_1, e_1)$ and $G_2 = (X_2, *_2, e_2)$ be groups.\nWe verify the group axioms for the direct product $G_1 \\times G_2$:\n1. **Associativity**: Let $(a_1, a_2), (b_1, b_2), (c_1, c_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2$.\n   By definition of the coordinatewise operation $*_3$:\n   $$ ((a_1, a_2) *_3 (b_1, b_2)) *_3 (c_1, c_2) = (a_1 *_1 b_1, a_2 *_2 b_2) *_3 (c_1, c_2) = ((a_1 *_1 b_1) *_1 c_1, (a_2 *_2 b_2) *_2 c_2). $$\n   By the associativity of $*_1$ in $G_1$ and $*_2$ in $G_2$, this equals:\n   $$ (a_1 *_1 (b_1 *_1 c_1), a_2 *_2 (b_2 *_2 c_2)) = (a_1, a_2) *_3 (b_1 *_1 c_1, b_2 *_2 c_2) = (a_1, a_2) *_3 ((b_1, b_2) *_3 (c_1, c_2)). $$\n2. **Identity**: The element $e_3 = (e_1, e_2)$ satisfies:\n   $$ (a_1, a_2) *_3 (e_1, e_2) = (a_1 *_1 e_1, a_2 *_2 e_2) = (a_1, a_2), $$\n   and symmetrically $(e_1, e_2) *_3 (a_1, a_2) = (a_1, a_2)$.\n3. **Inverses**: For any $(a_1, a_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2$, the element $(a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1})$ satisfies:\n   $$ (a_1, a_2) *_3 (a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1}) = (a_1 *_1 a_1^{-1}, a_2 *_2 a_2^{-1}) = (e_1, e_2) = e_3, $$\n   and $(a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1}) *_3 (a_1, a_2) = (e_1, e_2) = e_3$.\nThus $(G_1 \\times G_2, *_3, e_3)$ is a group.\n\"\"\"",
  "page": null,
  "source": "Theorem:\ngiven: G1, G2 is \\group\nthen: G1 \\.group.direct.product./ G2 is? \\group\nDocumented:\n. called: \"Direct Product of Groups is a Group\"\nProof: \"\"\"\nLet $G_1 = (X_1, *_1, e_1)$ and $G_2 = (X_2, *_2, e_2)$ be groups.\nWe verify the group axioms for the direct product $G_1 \\times G_2$:\n1. **Associativity**: Let $(a_1, a_2), (b_1, b_2), (c_1, c_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2$.\n   By definition of the coordinatewise operation $*_3$:\n   $$ ((a_1, a_2) *_3 (b_1, b_2)) *_3 (c_1, c_2) = (a_1 *_1 b_1, a_2 *_2 b_2) *_3 (c_1, c_2) = ((a_1 *_1 b_1) *_1 c_1, (a_2 *_2 b_2) *_2 c_2). $$\n   By the associativity of $*_1$ in $G_1$ and $*_2$ in $G_2$, this equals:\n   $$ (a_1 *_1 (b_1 *_1 c_1), a_2 *_2 (b_2 *_2 c_2)) = (a_1, a_2) *_3 (b_1 *_1 c_1, b_2 *_2 c_2) = (a_1, a_2) *_3 ((b_1, b_2) *_3 (c_1, c_2)). $$\n2. **Identity**: The element $e_3 = (e_1, e_2)$ satisfies:\n   $$ (a_1, a_2) *_3 (e_1, e_2) = (a_1 *_1 e_1, a_2 *_2 e_2) = (a_1, a_2), $$\n   and symmetrically $(e_1, e_2) *_3 (a_1, a_2) = (a_1, a_2)$.\n3. **Inverses**: For any $(a_1, a_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2$, the element $(a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1})$ satisfies:\n   $$ (a_1, a_2) *_3 (a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1}) = (a_1 *_1 a_1^{-1}, a_2 *_2 a_2^{-1}) = (e_1, e_2) = e_3, $$\n   and $(a_1^{-1}, a_2^{-1}) *_3 (a_1, a_2) = (e_1, e_2) = e_3$.\nThus $(G_1 \\times G_2, *_3, e_3)$ is a group.\n\"\"\"\nId: \"d65025cf-8bf1-454f-9507-55890ab9f88c\"",
